  "output": "Based on the code shown, here's a focused performance analysis with concrete fixes.\n\n## 1. Caching opportunities\n\n**`src/utils/formatting.ts` — `parseApiDate` regex runs redundantly**\n`formatDate`/`formatTime`/`timeAgo` all call `parseApiDate` separately even when passed the same value repeatedly in list renders (e.g., transcript tables calling both `formatDate` and `timeAgo` on the same `created_at`). Memoize per-value:\n\n```ts\nconst dateCache = new Map<string, Date>();\nexport function parseApiDate(value: string) {\n  const cached = dateCache.get(value);\n  if (cached) return cached;\n  const hasTimezone = /(?:Z|[+-]\\d{2}:?\\d{2})$/i.test(value);\n  const date = new Date(hasTimezone ? value : `${value}Z`);\n  dateCache.set(value, date);\n  return date;\n}\n```\nCap the map size or use a small LRU if transcript volume is large.\n\n**`src/api.ts` — no response caching for reference/list data**\n`request<T>()` hits the network on every call with no ETag/cache-control handling. Static-ish data (phrases, outcomes, language settings) gets refetched on every view mount. Add a simple TTL cache keyed by path for GET requests, reusing the pattern already in `utils/cache.ts`:\n\n```ts\nconst memCache = new Map<string, { data: unknown; expires: number }>();\nasync function cachedGet<T>(path: string, ttlMs = 30_000): Promise<T> {\n  const hit = memCache.get(path);\n  if (hit && hit.expires > Date.now()) return hit.data as T;\n  const data = await request<T>(path);\n  memCache.set(path, { data, expires: Date.now() + ttlMs });\n  return data;\n}\n```\n\n## 2. Memory leaks\n\n**`src/hooks/useCountUp.ts` — `requestAnimationFrame` not cancelled on unmount**\nThe `tick` recursion via `requestAnimationFrame` has no cleanup — if the component unmounts mid-animation, `setValue`/`ref.current` calls continue against a stale closure (React will warn, and the rAF chain keeps running until `progress >= 1`).\n\n```ts\nuseEffect(() => {\n  if (target === 0) { setValue(0); return; }\n  let cancelled = false;\n  let rafId: number;\n  const start = performance.now();\n  ref.current?.classList.add('counting');\n  const tick = (now: number) => {\n    if (cancelled) return;\n    const progress = Math.min((now - start) / duration, 1);\n    const eased = 1 - Math.pow(1 - progress, 3);\n    setValue(Math.round(target * eased));\n    if (progress < 1) rafId = requestAnimationFrame(tick);\n    else ref.current?.classList.remove('counting');\n  };\n  rafId = requestAnimationFrame(tick);\n  return () => { cancelled = true; cancelAnimationFrame(rafId); };\n}, [target, duration]);\n```\n\n**`src/hooks/useToasts.ts` — dangling `setTimeout` on unmount**\n`showToast`'s `setTimeout(() => dismissToast(id), durationMs)` isn't tracked or cleared. If the component using this hook unmounts before the timer fires, `setState` runs on an unmounted component. Track timers in a ref and clear them in a cleanup effect:\n\n```ts\nconst timers = useRef<Set<ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>>>(new Set());\n// in showToast:\nif (durationMs > 0) {\n  const t = setTimeout(() => { dismissToast(id); timers.current.delete(t); }, durationMs);\n  timers.current.add(t);\n}\n// add a useEffect that clears timers.current on unmount\n```\n\n## 3. Redundant computations\n\n**`src/utils/transcript.ts` — `buildConversationItems` re-filters/re-sorts on every call**\n`events.filter(...)`, three separate `eventItems.filter(...)` passes, and a full array sort run on every render if the caller doesn't memoize (likely in a view rendering the transcript detail panel). Wrap the call site in `useMemo(() => buildConversationItems(transcript, events), [transcript, events])` rather than fixing the util itself — it's a pure function, the caller is the fix point. Also collapse the three separate `eventItems.filter` passes into a single `reduce` grouping by START/END/other to avoid 3x array traversal:\n\n```ts\nconst grouped = eventItems.reduce(\n  (acc, e) => {\n    if (e.kind === 'event' && START_EVENT_TYPES.has(e.title)) acc.start.push(e);\n    else if (e.kind === 'event' && END_EVENT_TYPES.has(e.title)) acc.end.push(e);\n    else acc.mid.push(e);\n    return acc;\n  },\n  { start: [] as ConversationItem[], mid: [] as ConversationItem[], end: [] as ConversationItem[] }\n);\nreturn [...grouped.start, ...turnItems, ...grouped.mid, ...grouped.end];\n```\n\n## 4. Unnecessary React re-renders\n\nThe truncated `App.tsx`/view files aren't fully visible, but two patterns are visible from prior graph findings (obs 1389, 1395) and worth flagging even without full file content:\n\n- **`TranscriptsView`** reportedly re-renders unfiltered rows without memoization — wrap row components in `React.memo` and derive the filtered list via `useMemo` keyed on the filter/search state, not on every parent render.\n- **Campaign strategy objects rebuilt on every render** — any `dialing_strategy` object literal built inline in JSX props (e.g. `<Foo strategy={{ ...campaign.dialing_strategy, x }} />`) will break `React.memo`/`useEffect` deps downstream. Hoist with `useMemo([campaign.dialing_strategy])`.\n\nI don't have those view files in context here — if you want, I can pull them via the code-review graph (`query_graph`/`get_review_context`) to give line-specific fixes instead of pattern-based guidance.\n\n## 5. N+1 patterns\n\nNone visible in the frontend files shown here (this is a fetch-based SPA, not an ORM context) — N+1 issues live in `callback_worker` (already flagged in prior session: obs 1380, \"Multiple Database Roundtrips Per Document\"). Want me to pull that file and give concrete fixes there too?\n\n**Priority order:** the two memory leaks (`useCountUp`, `useToasts`) are cheap, safe fixes — I'd do those now. Want me to apply them directly?\n",
